WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.250
Then nav modifiers, we've already touched on this a little bit,

2
00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:08.890
but just to actually show you them being implemented very simply.

3
00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:13.810
The easiest way to introduce nav modifiers is if we were to,

4
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:15.730
they can have an independent volume,

5
00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:17.850
so nav modifier volume here,

6
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:22.989
and you can see just by putting it here,

7
00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:25.269
this is blocking off the geometry.

8
00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:28.690
When you create a nav modifier volume,

9
00:00:28.929 --> 00:00:31.010
you get to choose an area class.

10
00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:34.109
You can create your own here if you need to do something slightly different.

11
00:00:34.109 --> 00:00:36.229
But by default, we have a null,

12
00:00:36.229 --> 00:00:40.850
which is this, I'm blocking off this space as non-navigable.

13
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:42.630
But we can also do obstacle,

14
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.329
which is essentially an area of high cost.

15
00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:49.729
What you'll see is if I were to put this nav modifier volume here,

16
00:00:49.729 --> 00:00:53.570
when I tried to get my agent to navigate from here to this point,

17
00:00:53.570 --> 00:01:00.170
it's actually going to navigate around that space.

18
00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:08.760
If I were to put this like this,

19
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.840
you see it goes really far out of the way to navigate around that space,

20
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.319
because we've marked this as high cost.

21
00:01:16.319 --> 00:01:18.719
Nav modifier volumes just give you

22
00:01:18.719 --> 00:01:22.540
a very easy way to introduce the idea of high cost areas,

23
00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.640
where the AI will choose it if there's no other path.

24
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.659
It lets you define spaces that are completely non-navigable,

25
00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:36.859
even though there is no other collision geometry that would block that.

26
00:01:37.099 --> 00:01:40.939
It gives us the capacity to change our nav mesh.

27
00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:42.900
The other thing to mention here,

28
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:51.500
we already talked about the idea of having possibly dynamic nav mesh.

29
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:56.760
If I were to go to runtime,

30
00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.159
if I just move our nav modifier out here,

31
00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:03.719
and I were to just play,

32
00:02:03.719 --> 00:02:11.979
and then I eject and I find my nav modifier volume,

33
00:02:11.979 --> 00:02:14.219
you'll just have to trust me that it's there.

34
00:02:14.219 --> 00:02:15.379
Unfortunately, we don't get

35
00:02:15.379 --> 00:02:17.300
the editor handles when we're in play and editor mode.

36
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.099
If I were to snap that down to zero,

37
00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:24.300
you can see that that's actually carving out this space at runtime.

38
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.219
The reason that's working is because our nav mesh modifier

39
00:02:29.219 --> 00:02:31.979
is set to dynamic modifiers only.

40
00:02:31.979 --> 00:02:36.099
If I change this to static and then I go to play again,

41
00:02:36.099 --> 00:02:42.889
same thing, go to nav modifier volume,

42
00:02:42.889 --> 00:02:45.129
and I were to snap that to zero,

43
00:02:45.129 --> 00:02:46.689
you'd see our nav mesh doesn't change,

44
00:02:46.689 --> 00:02:48.289
and that's because it's now in static mode,

45
00:02:48.289 --> 00:02:50.849
so it's ignoring any dynamic changes.

46
00:02:50.849 --> 00:02:54.849
That is a unique power given to nav modifiers,

47
00:02:54.849 --> 00:02:58.370
is the fact that we have this second or third state

48
00:02:58.409 --> 00:03:01.490
for our nav mesh where we can be fully dynamic

49
00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:03.650
and then obviously would pay attention to modifiers,

50
00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:06.729
or we can ignore other kinds of actors,

51
00:03:06.729 --> 00:03:08.610
only pay attention to nav modifiers,

52
00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.650
so we're gaining some of the performance benefits.

53
00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.250
We're not trying to consider every kind of actor,

54
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:18.169
but uniquely allow modifiers to change our mesh at runtime,

55
00:03:18.169 --> 00:03:21.090
and then that's another area where nav modifiers and

56
00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:26.289
nav modifier volumes can give us extra functionality.

57
00:03:26.289 --> 00:03:30.360
Onto the environmental query system,

58
00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:34.759
we've talked a lot about how we're able to navigate space,

59
00:03:34.759 --> 00:03:38.240
but one of the important things when we're talking about

60
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.400
querying things about the environment,

61
00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.400
is the ability to ascertain where we even might want to go.

62
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.039
A typical thing that you might see in

63
00:03:49.039 --> 00:03:50.639
maybe a third person shooter,

64
00:03:50.639 --> 00:03:54.479
is you have an enemy who seeks out cover.

65
00:03:54.479 --> 00:03:57.479
So we've got the navigation which can get us to that cover,

66
00:03:57.479 --> 00:04:00.080
but how do we choose which cover to go to?

67
00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.199
That's somewhere where our EQS,

68
00:04:02.199 --> 00:04:04.599
the environmental query system, can come in.

69
00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.759
So the environmental query system allows AI to gather,

70
00:04:09.759 --> 00:04:11.879
well, not just AI really, anything in the game,

71
00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:18.439
and it is powerfully used by AI, but it isn't unique to AI.

72
00:04:18.439 --> 00:04:20.600
It can actually be used in gameplay as well.

73
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:24.160
It's basically a way to query things about the environment,

74
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:30.680
to construct, to ask questions about our environment,

75
00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.720
construct a bunch of tests that say,

76
00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.480
you know, I want to seek out the nearest suitable cover

77
00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:39.279
for this kind of enemy,

78
00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:42.639
and then choose the location based off that query.

79
00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:46.550
It's worth mentioning that whenever we're talking about EQS,

80
00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:49.730
it is blocked behind a plugin,

81
00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:51.889
so the environment query editor plugin.

82
00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:53.970
I think when you start a new project,

83
00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:55.730
usually this is enabled by default,

84
00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:57.930
but just to kind of highlight that,

85
00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:05.009
if we go to our plugins window and we do environment query editor,

86
00:05:05.009 --> 00:05:07.129
again, I think it's typically on by default,

87
00:05:07.129 --> 00:05:10.089
but just know that if some of this EQS functionality

88
00:05:10.089 --> 00:05:11.610
doesn't seem to be available to you,

89
00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.009
just make sure you've got this plugin enabled.

90
00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:23.079
And then yeah, essentially, kind of fundamentally,

91
00:05:23.079 --> 00:05:24.920
it lets us make these queries,

92
00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:29.079
and we use a combination of generating points,

93
00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.199
querying that, filtering and scoring

94
00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:34.360
to give either the best result

95
00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:37.439
or a couple of different options for either, you know,

96
00:05:37.439 --> 00:05:39.959
randomly choosing from the best 25%

97
00:05:39.959 --> 00:05:42.519
or the best 5%, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

98
00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:46.800
Yeah, environmental queries can be called from Behavior Tree,

99
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.800
can also be called from C++ and can be run in Blueprint,

100
00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:54.160
so it's not purely for AI,

101
00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:55.920
it can also be used for gameplay.

102
00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.160
I've used it previously, for example,

103
00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.120
when generating points to spawn enemies at,

104
00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.600
or points where you might want to spawn drops,

105
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.079
maybe you don't want to spawn your drops

106
00:06:08.079 --> 00:06:10.040
on top of any pre-existing enemies,

107
00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.740
you could use an EQS to kind of evaluate that.

108
00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:17.019
So the three key components that go into making up

109
00:06:17.019 --> 00:06:22.680
the EQS system are generators, contexts, and tests.

110
00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:26.519
Generators are essentially the thing, the first step,

111
00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:28.120
they're the thing that we say,

112
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:34.220
the kind of fundamental makeup of an EQS query

113
00:06:34.939 --> 00:06:36.660
is a carpet of points.

114
00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:38.699
We essentially generate a carpet of points

115
00:06:38.699 --> 00:06:41.379
around our world.

116
00:06:41.379 --> 00:06:43.899
It's the generator that's actually used to define

117
00:06:43.899 --> 00:06:45.899
where should this carpet of points be,

118
00:06:45.899 --> 00:06:47.579
how should they be laid out?

119
00:06:47.579 --> 00:06:49.980
So we have a couple of different classes available to us

120
00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.740
by default, we can generate those points in a circle,

121
00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:55.579
which is just, we define like a radius

122
00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:59.060
and then a, you know, degrees around

123
00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:02.220
or a kind of radians around, not even radians,

124
00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:05.500
centimeters around the circumference of that circle.

125
00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.899
We can do a grid where we specify essentially

126
00:07:07.899 --> 00:07:12.860
a kind of 2D cube, a 2D square,

127
00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:17.300
and we say every 50 centimeters wide

128
00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:21.220
and every 10 centimeters in depth generate a new point.

129
00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:22.139
And then we have a donut,

130
00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:23.540
which is a little bit similar to our circle,

131
00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:26.060
but it's the idea that we might have multiple rings.

132
00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:28.139
So we kind of generate in rows.

133
00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.139
Yeah, we can talk about, we can look at each of these

134
00:07:31.139 --> 00:07:32.899
kind of actually in the editor,

135
00:07:34.139 --> 00:07:35.339
but we have a couple of different ways

136
00:07:35.339 --> 00:07:37.060
of generating these points.

137
00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:39.740
And they are essentially, again,

138
00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:42.100
used to produce these locations

139
00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.500
and it's this kind of carpet of points.

140
00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.620
And it's then that carpet of points

141
00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.300
that we perform the tests on

142
00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:53.410
to actually give us our final result from the query.

143
00:07:53.649 --> 00:07:55.970
And also, yeah, custom generators can be created

144
00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.689
in Blueprint and C++ if, you know,

145
00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:02.089
circle, grid, donut aren't unsuitable to what we need.

146
00:08:02.089 --> 00:08:03.930
Contexts are an important element.

147
00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:05.089
They're neither, the kind of,

148
00:08:05.089 --> 00:08:06.730
the generators are what generate the points

149
00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:08.930
and the tests are what test those points.

150
00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:11.050
But the contexts are things that are used

151
00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:11.970
at various points,

152
00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:14.529
either within the generators or the tests.

153
00:08:14.529 --> 00:08:16.689
They're a kind of frame of reference.

154
00:08:17.569 --> 00:08:20.769
So you'll see as we kind of quickly diagnose

155
00:08:20.769 --> 00:08:23.170
some preexisting EQSs that I've made,

156
00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.810
the kind of various points at which we can use

157
00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:27.529
the various contexts,

158
00:08:27.529 --> 00:08:32.210
but they kind of give us a, yeah, a frame of reference.

159
00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:36.809
A context can return either a single entity or an array,

160
00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:39.850
but it's just important to mention

161
00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:43.129
that not all things that might be used in that context

162
00:08:43.129 --> 00:08:47.730
are set up to handle a context that can return an array.

163
00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:51.009
Some of them are only set up to handle a context

164
00:08:51.009 --> 00:08:52.529
that returns a single item.

165
00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:57.490
So a context might be the actor

166
00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:00.370
actually creating the EQS query.

167
00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:03.009
That would be the kind of one of the standard

168
00:09:03.009 --> 00:09:04.690
query context is querier,

169
00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:07.889
the person who is actually making this query.

170
00:09:07.889 --> 00:09:10.769
Another example of a context might be

171
00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:15.299
all the rooms in your dungeon.

172
00:09:15.299 --> 00:09:18.100
You might create a custom context that says,

173
00:09:18.139 --> 00:09:23.139
oh, use my room logic to return an array

174
00:09:23.139 --> 00:09:25.220
of all the rooms in my dungeon.

175
00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:27.860
And then I can use all the rooms in my dungeon

176
00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:31.059
around the generator or the tests for various reasons.

177
00:09:31.059 --> 00:09:33.220
So again, these contexts are kind of

178
00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:35.059
between the lines here,

179
00:09:35.059 --> 00:09:36.980
the generators and the tests are the key points

180
00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:40.440
and the context can be used in each of these situations.

181
00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:42.559
And finally, we have the tests themselves,

182
00:09:42.559 --> 00:09:45.279
which are actually used to determine

183
00:09:45.279 --> 00:09:49.799
which these tests are gonna basically run

184
00:09:51.399 --> 00:09:54.480
filters and scoring on each of these points

185
00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.519
that the generator has generated

186
00:09:56.519 --> 00:09:59.919
to establish whether or not this particular

187
00:09:59.919 --> 00:10:02.799
and individual point should pass the test.

188
00:10:02.799 --> 00:10:06.600
And if it does pass, if it does not get filtered out,

189
00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.279
how should it be scored?

190
00:10:08.279 --> 00:10:11.080
So if we were to, the easiest thing to do now

191
00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:14.000
is actually just quickly jump into an example.

192
00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:16.679
So if we go, this should be in your project

193
00:10:16.679 --> 00:10:18.720
if you've downloaded that.

194
00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:20.320
So in the EQS folder,

195
00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:25.320
we've got two different EQSs that I've kind of made here.

196
00:10:26.879 --> 00:10:31.320
So as we can see, I've made a doughnut

197
00:10:34.399 --> 00:10:37.960
and this is just gonna kind of generate

198
00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:41.759
several rings of points around our context.

199
00:10:41.759 --> 00:10:46.759
And as I mentioned, our center is defined as an actual,

200
00:10:48.559 --> 00:10:50.159
this is where we would use our context.

201
00:10:50.159 --> 00:10:52.639
So we have, our doughnut is using

202
00:10:52.639 --> 00:10:54.320
a custom context that I've made,

203
00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:56.639
which is based off a target actor key,

204
00:10:56.639 --> 00:10:58.639
so a blackboard key,

205
00:10:58.639 --> 00:11:00.799
rather than the person who's asking the query,

206
00:11:00.799 --> 00:11:03.879
this is intended to generate points around the person

207
00:11:03.879 --> 00:11:06.600
who we want to attack, essentially.

208
00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.279
So that's coming from a blackboard key

209
00:11:09.279 --> 00:11:13.899
and that's what I've made a custom context for that.

210
00:11:13.899 --> 00:11:17.620
And then we've got our two, these are our tests.

211
00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:20.100
So our doughnut is gonna generate

212
00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:22.460
around this particular context.

213
00:11:26.139 --> 00:11:27.980
And then that's gonna generate

214
00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:30.500
based on what our doughnut says.

215
00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:32.539
So we've got an inner radius and outer radius

216
00:11:32.539 --> 00:11:33.940
and how many rings we need to generate.

217
00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:37.419
So we're gonna start at a radius of 100

218
00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:41.220
and we're gonna generate three rings between 100 and 200.

219
00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:43.820
And every ring is gonna have eight points.

220
00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:45.860
And this is, that's just our initial generation.

221
00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:48.659
And then we're gonna run these tests

222
00:11:48.659 --> 00:11:50.620
on each of those points that we've generated.

223
00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:52.340
So, you know, three lots of eights,

224
00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:54.059
we're gonna have 24 points.

225
00:11:55.019 --> 00:11:58.500
First thing we're gonna do is this path exists a test.

226
00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:01.059
This is a pre-existing test that exists in the engine.

227
00:12:01.059 --> 00:12:04.379
This is essentially say, can we nav to this point?

228
00:12:04.379 --> 00:12:07.379
And then secondly, what is our distance from the point?

229
00:12:07.379 --> 00:12:10.460
So as I said, tests can be either

230
00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:12.659
a test that filters points out,

231
00:12:12.659 --> 00:12:16.220
or it can be a test that scores them, or it can do both.

232
00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:18.860
So in this situation, our path exists,

233
00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:20.580
purely filters them out.

234
00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:23.740
It just says, if we can nav to this point,

235
00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:26.500
if this point is on navigable space,

236
00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:28.259
then it would pass the filter.

237
00:12:28.259 --> 00:12:30.740
If not, then it fails the filter.

238
00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:33.700
And then this next test that we do is based off distance.

239
00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:37.659
This hypothetically could filter points

240
00:12:37.659 --> 00:12:41.740
if it's too far or not far enough, and it could score them.

241
00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:44.340
But in the context of this distance 2D,

242
00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:48.340
which is again another pre-existing filter,

243
00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:50.179
I've chosen to score it only.

244
00:12:50.179 --> 00:12:52.980
So I'm not gonna filter any points using this test,

245
00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:54.580
I'm only gonna score them.

246
00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:59.460
And I've said it should score based off 2D distance,

247
00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:00.299
not 3D distance,

248
00:13:00.299 --> 00:13:02.460
so I don't want to care about verticality here.

249
00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.379
And then I've just set some things

250
00:13:04.379 --> 00:13:05.700
about our scoring equations,

251
00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:09.340
so that this is essentially saying that

252
00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:11.779
if we think that distance from the querier

253
00:13:11.779 --> 00:13:14.740
is gonna go up, that number is gonna get bigger,

254
00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:18.340
so the default scoring is gonna be further away is better.

255
00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:20.139
So by inversing that scoring,

256
00:13:20.139 --> 00:13:21.820
I'm essentially saying closer is better.

257
00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:26.460
So I want a point that is closer to our querier.

258
00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:30.179
So a point, the querier being the person asking the EQS,

259
00:13:30.179 --> 00:13:33.779
so in this context, it's the AI asking the question,

260
00:13:33.779 --> 00:13:35.419
I want a point that is closer

261
00:13:35.419 --> 00:13:37.960
to the person actually making the query.

262
00:13:39.759 --> 00:13:42.360
So we can see here how we've used generators,

263
00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.639
that generator has used a context.

264
00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:51.840
Our path exists, test has also used a context here.

265
00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:54.840
So the context here being that standard default context

266
00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:56.919
of the person who is actually making the query,

267
00:13:56.919 --> 00:13:58.340
so that's our AI.

268
00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:02.779
And this is asking whether a path exists

269
00:14:02.779 --> 00:14:05.379
to the point from this thing.

270
00:14:05.379 --> 00:14:08.659
And similarly, our distance, we've used a context again

271
00:14:08.659 --> 00:14:09.820
to say, I want the distance

272
00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:12.019
from the person asking the question.

273
00:14:12.019 --> 00:14:14.299
I could alternatively change the context

274
00:14:14.299 --> 00:14:16.299
to be our target actor,

275
00:14:16.299 --> 00:14:18.700
and then that changes the meaning of this distance.

276
00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:23.580
Then I'm asking, what is the distance from the,

277
00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:28.580
I want the point that is closest to the actual target.

278
00:14:29.019 --> 00:14:31.000
Whereas this is saying, I want to choose the point

279
00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:36.000
that is closest to the person asking the question

280
00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:39.250
to myself, the AI.

281
00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:41.309
So a kind of important thing to,

282
00:14:41.309 --> 00:14:44.690
it's all good to talk about all of this in context,

283
00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:46.809
in kind of like broad concepts,

284
00:14:46.809 --> 00:14:50.590
but when we're working with this,

285
00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:52.070
the environmental query system,

286
00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:53.110
obviously at some point,

287
00:14:53.289 --> 00:14:55.190
it's helpful to actually see some of this stuff, right?

288
00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:56.990
Because it all seems so abstract.

289
00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:59.350
So one of the important things to remember

290
00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:01.990
is that there is a very, very powerful Pawn

291
00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:03.750
that we can utilize to kind of visualize

292
00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:06.909
some of this stuff, and that's the EQS testing Pawn.

293
00:15:06.909 --> 00:15:10.549
The way to put one of these in your level

294
00:15:10.549 --> 00:15:14.389
is similar to our NavLink proxy.

295
00:15:14.389 --> 00:15:17.070
I don't think you can just find it unfortunately

296
00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:22.379
through the normal place actors.

297
00:15:22.419 --> 00:15:24.779
So there is probably some other way to navigate to it,

298
00:15:24.779 --> 00:15:27.500
but the way I typically do that is just I go to all,

299
00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:32.500
and then just search for EQS Pawn,

300
00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:35.139
and EQS testing Pawn comes up.

301
00:15:35.139 --> 00:15:37.419
You can just drag this into your level.

302
00:15:37.419 --> 00:15:38.620
And then this is just a Pawn.

303
00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:40.620
You'll notice whenever you do that,

304
00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:44.679
you'll get a, it will bring in its own AI controller.

305
00:15:44.679 --> 00:15:46.419
So if you need to delete this,

306
00:15:46.419 --> 00:15:49.139
when you go to delete your EQS testing Pawn,

307
00:15:49.139 --> 00:15:50.639
you'll get a complaint from your level,

308
00:15:50.679 --> 00:15:52.960
because this EQS testing Pawn

309
00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:55.159
is being possessed by this controller.

310
00:15:55.159 --> 00:15:59.019
So if and when you do go to delete your EQS testing Pawn,

311
00:15:59.019 --> 00:16:01.000
just be aware it brings in its own controller

312
00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:03.039
and you need to delete both.

313
00:16:03.039 --> 00:16:04.519
But what you do with this Pawn

314
00:16:04.519 --> 00:16:07.240
is you essentially just say,

315
00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:11.399
you give it an EQS to visualize, to test.

316
00:16:11.399 --> 00:16:13.360
So you can choose from any of the EQS's

317
00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:15.240
you've already made.

318
00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:17.840
And you can see that this would essentially

319
00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:20.039
just generate this carpet of points.

320
00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:22.039
Now, this isn't gonna be perfectly set up

321
00:16:22.039 --> 00:16:25.639
because this is relying on,

322
00:16:25.639 --> 00:16:28.240
if we actually, again, go and look at this,

323
00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:31.009
go and look at this thing.

324
00:16:31.009 --> 00:16:32.730
It's actually, this context here

325
00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:36.309
is relying on this EQC target actor key.

326
00:16:36.309 --> 00:16:41.309
So that this context is reading from a key

327
00:16:41.649 --> 00:16:43.429
from our blackboard,

328
00:16:43.429 --> 00:16:47.950
which is relevant at runtime for this particular AI.

329
00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:50.509
But in the context of in editor time,

330
00:16:50.509 --> 00:16:51.830
this might not always be suitable.

331
00:16:51.830 --> 00:16:54.629
So it's worth kind of bearing that kind of stuff in mind

332
00:16:54.629 --> 00:16:57.909
that when you're doing custom contexts,

333
00:16:57.909 --> 00:16:59.389
you need to be aware that if you want

334
00:16:59.389 --> 00:17:02.389
to be able to debug this properly at editor time,

335
00:17:02.389 --> 00:17:04.430
it might be worth kind of accounting for that

336
00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:06.950
and adding in some kind of default behavior.

337
00:17:06.950 --> 00:17:09.190
So if I were, the way I can kind of like,

338
00:17:09.190 --> 00:17:12.150
actually do that,

339
00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:16.579
I can just quickly demonstrate how we might do that

340
00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:21.380
is I will just quickly do query a blackboard.

341
00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:25.900
But what I'll also do is if that value is,

342
00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:27.380
for whatever reason, null,

343
00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:32.390
if I'll check if that is null,

344
00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:34.130
you can see it defaulting to just say

345
00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:35.849
around the actor itself.

346
00:17:35.849 --> 00:17:37.950
What I'll change this to is instead,

347
00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:47.299
I'll do get all actors of class.

348
00:17:47.299 --> 00:17:49.839
So this is the custom,

349
00:17:49.839 --> 00:17:55.990
this is the actual in custom context that I've made.

350
00:17:56.349 --> 00:17:59.109
This is the actor that represents that.

351
00:17:59.109 --> 00:18:00.829
And you can see, not actor, sorry,

352
00:18:00.829 --> 00:18:02.809
the class that represents that.

353
00:18:02.809 --> 00:18:05.210
And you can see it's got these two functions

354
00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:08.269
for provide single actor and provide single location,

355
00:18:08.269 --> 00:18:09.950
and similarly provide actors set

356
00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:12.829
and provide location set if you want to return an array.

357
00:18:13.789 --> 00:18:17.190
So I'm just going to get all actors of class,

358
00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:21.779
and I'm just going to look for character,

359
00:18:21.779 --> 00:18:24.519
and I'm just going to return the first character

360
00:18:24.519 --> 00:18:25.559
that we can find.

361
00:18:27.579 --> 00:18:29.500
Better yet, actually, my player here

362
00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:44.329
is represented by the BPAI player char.

363
00:18:44.329 --> 00:18:46.950
So I'll just quickly hook that up

364
00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:49.029
and then get actor location.

365
00:18:49.029 --> 00:18:52.430
Obviously this particular setup wouldn't work

366
00:18:52.430 --> 00:18:54.109
if there isn't one of those in your level,

367
00:18:54.109 --> 00:18:55.349
but this is just to demonstrate

368
00:18:55.349 --> 00:18:57.829
how you might handle this in a way

369
00:18:57.829 --> 00:19:02.829
that kind of appropriately visualizes in editor.

370
00:19:04.819 --> 00:19:09.009
One second, why is that not,

371
00:19:09.009 --> 00:19:10.930
is that actually finding the key?

372
00:19:12.869 --> 00:19:19.630
Maybe I just need to reassign that.

373
00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:20.630
Yeah, there we go.

374
00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:23.990
So obviously we don't know that this would be the target,

375
00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:25.829
but just to kind of give us

376
00:19:25.829 --> 00:19:27.869
a slightly better demonstration

377
00:19:27.869 --> 00:19:29.750
of how this would actually visualize

378
00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:34.069
if we don't seem to be finding this blackboard key

379
00:19:34.069 --> 00:19:35.349
on our blackboard, which we won't

380
00:19:35.349 --> 00:19:36.430
because we're not on runtime,

381
00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:38.390
so that blackboard key hasn't been assigned.

382
00:19:38.390 --> 00:19:41.269
We're just going to fall back to finding,

383
00:19:41.269 --> 00:19:43.609
trying to find a player in the level

384
00:19:43.609 --> 00:19:44.450
and getting the first one

385
00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:48.319
and pretending that that acts location is our target.

386
00:19:48.319 --> 00:19:50.319
So then that gives us a kind of

387
00:19:50.319 --> 00:19:51.759
slightly better visualization

388
00:19:51.759 --> 00:19:54.200
of what this EQS is actually going to do.

389
00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:57.200
It's going to generate points around our target,

390
00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:00.599
but then it's going to score our points

391
00:20:00.599 --> 00:20:03.319
based off whether or not we can,

392
00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:07.680
based on what is closest to the person making the query,

393
00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:09.960
which in this case is the EQS testing porn.

394
00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:12.900
And then we can also see what happens

395
00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:15.240
if we move our actor here.

396
00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:18.839
We can see it's going to, maybe I need to refresh that.

397
00:20:22.339 --> 00:20:24.180
You can see that filtering taking place.

398
00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:26.380
So this is kind of important thing to bear in mind

399
00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:27.880
with the EQS testing porn,

400
00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:30.599
is that these red nodes with zero

401
00:20:30.599 --> 00:20:33.500
don't necessarily mean they're never going to be considered.

402
00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:37.319
If you're doing an EQS query where every point is valid,

403
00:20:37.319 --> 00:20:39.440
then this is still a valid point.

404
00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:41.519
It's just getting a score of zero.

405
00:20:41.519 --> 00:20:43.880
The only points that are actually getting filtered out

406
00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:45.960
are these hard dark blue ones.

407
00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:49.720
So the scoring mechanism goes from red to green

408
00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:52.160
based off how high the score is.

409
00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:56.039
The points that are hard getting filtered out

410
00:20:56.039 --> 00:20:58.759
by one of the filters are this dark blue.

411
00:20:58.759 --> 00:21:00.920
And they, rather than showing the score

412
00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:03.400
that they're receiving, they'll show a,

413
00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:05.599
they'll print out a bit of text to say

414
00:21:05.599 --> 00:21:09.930
which of these tests was actually filtering this point out.

415
00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:11.710
And that brings us back to kind of one last thing

416
00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:13.789
to bear in mind when you're kind of structuring

417
00:21:13.789 --> 00:21:17.130
these EQS queries is that it's good

418
00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:20.930
to be doing your filters first, essentially.

419
00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:24.490
If you can, going from like a top to bottom,

420
00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:26.849
if you're kind of putting your filters first,

421
00:21:26.849 --> 00:21:29.089
that means that your scoring queries

422
00:21:29.089 --> 00:21:31.009
won't be trying to score items

423
00:21:31.009 --> 00:21:32.890
that are never going to be considered

424
00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:35.289
because a filter later is going to filter them out anyway.

425
00:21:35.289 --> 00:21:36.650
So that's just kind of like a little thing

426
00:21:36.650 --> 00:21:40.569
to bear in mind when you're making these.

427
00:21:40.569 --> 00:21:42.970
But either way, you can see kind of by

428
00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:46.730
what's been demonstrated there that that's just a way

429
00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:48.650
to make an kind of intelligent decisions.

430
00:21:48.650 --> 00:21:50.670
You can imagine the same thing.

431
00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:53.769
As I said, I've done this for games in the past

432
00:21:53.769 --> 00:21:56.690
where I've used this to query all enemies

433
00:21:56.690 --> 00:22:00.670
in your current room and find a point that is,

434
00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:02.970
and then score them based off their distance

435
00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:04.369
from all of those enemies.

436
00:22:04.369 --> 00:22:06.210
So you can find a point that is most likely

437
00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:08.609
to not have an enemy currently overlapping it.

438
00:22:08.609 --> 00:22:11.650
And that's where we should spawn our item chest,

439
00:22:12.849 --> 00:22:13.849
for example.

440
00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:16.990
So you can use that in kind of gameplay context.

441
00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:18.910
You can use it in AI contexts.

442
00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:23.250
Yeah, we'll actually come to another example

443
00:22:23.250 --> 00:22:26.529
from one of the Unreal templates in a little bit

444
00:22:26.529 --> 00:22:29.089
where we're going to kind of stitch all this stuff together.
